among logicians, one of the modes of syllogisms of the third <a class="xref" href="vol9.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a>, the premises of which are universal affirmatives, whilst the conclusion is a particular affirmative: thus,

Every body is divisible;
Every body is a substance;
Therefore some substance is divisible.

in Logic, a <a class="xref" href="../1771/vol3.html#article-MODE">mode</a> of syllogisms in the third <a class="xref" href="vol9.html#article-FIGURE">figure</a>, in which the <a class="xref" href="../1771/vol3.html#article-MAJOR">major</a> is an universal affirmative, and the <a class="xref" href="vol15.html#article-MINOR">minor</a> and conclusion particular affirmative <a class="xref" href="../1778/vol0.html#article-PROPOSITIONS">propositions</a>. For example,

All who serve God are kings;
Some who serve God are <a class="xref" href="../1771/vol0.html#article-POOR">poor</a>;
Therefore some who are poor are kings.
